lowing</strong>: Patrol your area, respond to packer requests within 3 minutes and replenish medicines and consumables before supplies run out.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Get medication control right</strong>: Supply only one medication strength and one matching stack of orders per packing table at a time, verifying the match before replenishing.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Protect the cold chain</strong>: Follow cold-chain procedures and Good Distribution Practice (GDP) requirements, returning temperature-controlled medicines to the fridge immediately when no longer needed.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Handle medicines safely</strong>: Use designated holding areas, never leave medicines unattended in walkways or packing areas, and immediately quarantine damaged or suspect stock for Shift Lead review.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Keep the floor safe and ready</strong>: Clear cardboard, keep stations tidy and exits unobstructed, remove immediate hazards and report concerns promptly. Operate equipment only within your training and authorisation.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Work as one team</strong>: Support fellow Warehouse Operatives, cover neighbouring areas during breaks and promptly flag stock discrepancies or rotation errors.</p></li></ul><h2>Need to have</h2><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Warehouse knowledge</strong>: You understand warehouse operations, stock control, Health &amp; Safety and GDP requirements, and put them into practice.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Attention to detail</strong>: You check medication strengths, order matches and storage conditions carefully. Accuracy is essential.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Ownership</strong>: You stay organised, anticipate what's needed and manage your area independently as priorities change.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><stron